# Deploy no longer uses deploy/.env. Use backend/.env for Flask, Neo4j, Mongo, LLM keys, etc.
#
# From this directory, pass that file so Compose can read build args (e.g. VITE_API_BASE_URL, CADDY_PUBLISH_PORT):
#
#   docker compose --env-file ../backend/.env up -d --build
#
# Podman (no docker-compose plugin): install a compose implementation, then use it from deploy/:
#   sudo dnf install -y epel-release
#   sudo dnf install -y podman-compose
#   podman-compose --env-file ../backend/.env up -d --build
# (Or install Docker Engine + docker-compose-plugin from Docker’s repo and use `docker compose` instead.)
#
# In backend/.env, set at least (examples for Neo4j/Mongo already on the host):
#
#   SITE_ADDRESS=https://ailegaladvisor.eraconnect.net
#   VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://ailegaladvisor.eraconnect.net
#   NEO4J_URI=bolt://host.docker.internal:7687
#   MONGO_URI=mongodb://host.docker.internal:27017
#
# See backend/.env.example for the full variable list.
#
# Shared nginx (other apps already on 80/443): add only this app — do not replace nginx.
# 1. In backend/.env:
#      CADDY_PUBLISH_PORT=8080
# 2. In deploy/docker-compose.yml comment out the Caddy line that publishes host :443 (when nginx keeps 443).
# 3. Add deploy/nginx-ailegaladvisor.conf.example to nginx (exact server_name for this host; wildcard DNS alone is not enough). If the URL still shows another app, see the “Debug” section in that file.
#
# Other coexistence options: CADDY_PUBLISH_PORT; check: ss -tlnp | grep -E ':80 |:443 '
